; 42a-nonassoc.kurt

; `nonassoc OP` declares an infix operator non-associative: chaining it
; three times in a row (`a OP b OP c`) becomes a `ParseError` instead of
; silently picking a side (left- or right-associating). Mutually exclusive
; with `flat` (41-flat.kurt) -- an operator that's inherently associative
; by construction can't also assert that chaining it is ambiguous.

infix "before" 20 20
bool "before" 0
var a, b, c

nonassoc "before"

; explicit grouping still works fine, either way:
parse (a before b) before c
parse a before (b before c)

; but writing it without parentheses is now rejected, since it's genuinely
; ambiguous which grouping was meant:
expect "ParseError"
    a before b before c

; without `nonassoc`, Kurt would have silently picked one association (in
; practice, left-to-right) instead of raising -- fine for a truly
; associative operator like `+`, misleading for one where left- and
; right-grouping mean different things.
